grep 'dll$' (passed through):
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n   e   .   d   l   l  \n
grep -v 'dll$':
   b   .   t   x   t  \r  \n   c  \r   d  \r  \n
grep -c $'\r': 3  grep -cP '\r$': 3
a CR inside a line is data: 1 0
-b -o (the real bytes): 9:txt
mapfile, no -t (passed through):
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n
$( ) keeps an inner CRLF:
   x  \r  \n   y
read, a CR with no LF after it:
   z  \r
sed s/dll$/X/:
   a   .   X  \r  \n   b  \r  \n
sed N, G:
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n   b  \r  \n
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n  \n   b  \r  \n  \n
sed 's/\r$//' (names the CR, dos2unix):
   a   .   d   l   l  \n   b  \n
sed -i keeps CRLF:
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n   B  \r  \n
sed a (text sed writes: LF):
   a   .   d   l   l  \r  \n   n   e   w  \n   b  \r  \n
awk /dll$/ (passed through):
   a       b   .   d   l   l  \r  \n
awk print $NF (what awk makes ends with LF):
   b   .   d   l   l  \n   d  \n
awk $2 = "X"; print (the record, edited, keeps its end):
   a       X  \r  \n   c       X  \r  \n
awk printf, END (what awk writes: LF):
   b   .   d   l   l   |   d   |   2  \n
awk sub(/\r$/, "") (names the CR):
   a       b   .   d   l   l  \n   c       d  \n
awk ORS set:
   a   ;   c   ;
cut -d' ' -f1 (what cut selects ends with LF):
   a  \n   d  \n
cut -c2-3:
       b  \n       e  \n
cut -f1 (no delimiter: the line whole):
   a       b       c  \r  \n   d       e  \n
rev (what rev writes ends with LF):
   c       b       a  \n   e       d  \n
uniq (the first line as it came):
   x  \r  \n   y  \n
sort (each line as it came):
   a  \n   b  \r  \n
nl (a CRLF blank line is not numbered):
                       1  \t   x  \r  \n                        
  \r  \n
